 CASEFILE: AI-Powered Missing Person Investigation and Location Prediction System

1. Abstract

CASEFILE is an academic machine learning project that analyzes GPS movement data and estimates probable geographical locations from historical movement patterns. The project uses machine learning techniques such as K-Means Clustering, Isolation Forest, Random Forest, and Markov Chain analysis.

The system performs data preprocessing, feature engineering, movement analysis, anomaly detection, location prediction, route analysis, priority scoring, model explainability, and interactive visualization. A Streamlit dashboard is also developed to display the results in an easy-to-understand format.

The project is developed for educational purposes using synthetic/public GPS data. It does not use real missing-person personal information, and its predictions are probabilistic estimates rather than proof of a person's actual location.

2. Introduction

GPS movement data can provide useful information about geographical and movement patterns. Machine learning can be used to analyze this data and identify frequently visited areas, unusual movement patterns, and possible future locations based on historical records.

The CASEFILE project demonstrates an end-to-end machine learning system that processes GPS data, performs movement analysis, predicts probable locations, analyzes routes, and presents the results through an interactive dashboard.

3. Problem Statement

Finding useful patterns from large amounts of GPS movement data manually can be difficult and time-consuming. There is a need for a system that can automatically process movement data, identify geographical patterns, detect unusual movements, estimate probable locations, and analyze movement routes.

The CASEFILE project addresses this problem by combining multiple machine learning and data analysis techniques into one academic system.

4. Objectives

The main objectives of the project are:

1. To collect and analyze GPS movement data.
2. To clean and preprocess the collected data.
3. To create useful movement-related features.
4. To identify geographical movement clusters using K-Means.
5. To detect unusual movement patterns using Isolation Forest.
6. To estimate probable locations using Random Forest.
7. To analyze location transitions using Markov Chain.
8. To calculate a demonstration priority score.
9. To provide model explainability using feature importance.
10. To display geographical information using an interactive map.
11. To develop a Streamlit dashboard for visualization.
12. To evaluate the machine learning model using different metrics.

5. Dataset

The project uses GPS movement data containing geographical coordinates and user movement information.

The dataset contains fields such as:

- User ID
- Date
- Time
- Latitude
- Longitude

A synthetic GPS dataset containing 500 records was created for the academic demonstration.

The data is used only for educational and machine learning experimentation.

6. Data Preprocessing

Data preprocessing was performed before applying machine learning algorithms.

The following preprocessing steps were used:

- Loading the GPS dataset.
- Removing duplicate records.
- Removing missing values.
- Converting date values into a proper date format.
- Converting time values into a proper time format.
- Sorting records according to user, date, and time.
- Encoding User IDs into numerical values for machine learning.

The cleaned dataset was saved as:

"data/cleaned_gps_data.csv"

7. Feature Engineering

Feature engineering was performed to create additional information from the original GPS data.

The following features were created:

- Hour
- Minute
- Day
- Month
- Weekday
- Weekend indicator
- Previous latitude
- Previous longitude
- Distance
- Speed

The distance between consecutive GPS records was calculated using the difference between latitude and longitude values.

These additional features help the machine learning models understand movement patterns.

The feature-engineered dataset was saved as:

"data/feature_engineered_gps.csv"

8. Exploratory Data Analysis

Exploratory Data Analysis was performed to understand the structure and characteristics of the GPS dataset.

The analysis focused on:

- Number of records.
- Latitude and longitude distribution.
- User movement patterns.
- Time-based movement information.
- Distance between consecutive locations.
- Speed-related information.

EDA helps in understanding the dataset before applying machine learning algorithms.

9. K-Means Clustering

K-Means clustering was used to group GPS coordinates into geographical clusters.

The latitude and longitude values were standardized before applying the algorithm.

The project used 5 clusters.

The purpose of clustering is to identify groups of locations that are geographically close to each other and understand common movement areas.

The clustering results were saved in:

"data/clustered_gps_data.csv"

The trained clustering model was saved as:

"clustering_model.pkl"

10. Anomaly Detection

Isolation Forest was used to identify unusual GPS movement patterns.

The model analyzes features such as:

- Latitude
- Longitude
- Distance
- Speed

The system classifies records as either:

- Normal
- Anomaly

An anomaly only indicates that a movement record is unusual compared with the other records. It does not automatically mean that the movement is suspicious or criminal.

The results were saved in:

"data/anomaly_detected_gps.csv"

The trained model was saved as:

"anomaly_model.pkl"

11. Location Prediction

Random Forest Regression was used to estimate probable latitude and longitude values.

The model uses features such as:

- User ID
- Hour
- Minute
- Day
- Month

The model was trained using historical GPS records.

The predicted latitude and longitude are displayed as estimated geographical coordinates.

The trained model was saved as:

"location_model.pkl"

The User ID encoder was saved as:

"user_encoder.pkl"

12. Route Prediction Using Markov Chain

Markov Chain analysis was used to study transitions between different geographical clusters.

Each cluster was treated as a location state.

The previous location and current location were compared to calculate transition probabilities.

For example, if a user frequently moves from Cluster 1 to Cluster 3, the transition probability between these locations becomes part of the route analysis.

The transition probability table was saved as:

"data/route_transition_probabilities.csv"

13. Priority Score

A demonstration priority score was developed by combining several factors.

The factors include:

- ML probability
- Historical frequency
- Route similarity
- Distance relevance
- Time relevance
- Anomaly evidence

The weighted components were combined to produce a score between 0 and 100.

The score was categorized as:

- Low
- Medium
- High
- Very High

This priority score is only a demonstration for the academic project and is not a validated real-world investigation scoring system.

The results were saved as:

"data/priority_scored_gps.csv"

14. Model Explainability

Model explainability was included to understand which input features contribute to the Random Forest prediction.

Feature importance was calculated from the trained Random Forest model.

The features analyzed include:

- User ID encoded
- Hour
- Minute
- Day
- Month

The feature importance results were saved as:

"data/feature_importance.csv"

Explainability helps users understand the factors considered by the model instead of treating the prediction as a black box.

15. Interactive Map

An interactive geographical map was created using Folium.

The map displays GPS records using latitude and longitude coordinates.

Information displayed on the map includes:

- User ID
- Latitude
- Longitude
- Cluster
- Movement status
- Priority level

The interactive map was saved as:

"data/casefile_map.html"

The map provides a visual representation of GPS movement patterns.

16. Streamlit Dashboard

A Streamlit dashboard was developed to provide an interactive interface for the project.

The dashboard contains:

- CASEFILE project title
- Case selection
- User ID selection
- Number of records
- Priority level
- Latest latitude
- Latest longitude
- Priority score
- Normal records
- Anomaly records
- GPS movement map
- Case data table

The dashboard allows users to select a User ID and view the corresponding movement information.

The dashboard is intended for academic demonstration.

17. Flask Prediction Interface

A Flask web application was also developed for location prediction.

The user can enter:

- User ID
- Date
- Time

The application processes the input and displays the predicted latitude and longitude.

The Flask interface provides a simple web-based way to demonstrate the location prediction model.

18. Model Evaluation

Model evaluation was performed to measure the performance of the location prediction model.

The evaluation includes regression metrics such as:

- Mean Absolute Error (MAE)
- Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)

Additional classification-style metrics were also explored for the academic demonstration:

- Accuracy
- Precision
- Recall
- F1 Score
- Confusion Matrix

Top-1, Top-3, and Top-5 location ranking evaluation were identified as useful future evaluation methods for a system that produces ranked location candidates.

Because the current model predicts continuous latitude and longitude values, classification-style metrics and Top-K ranking require a more suitable location-ranking implementation for rigorous evaluation.

